-## Limits
-
-- Practice target met means three fresh full-length runs met this trainer’s own target under the current clocks. Repeated-question runs and older runs without freshness checks are excluded. This is not a calibrated prediction of an Optiver result.
-- The battery changes between roles and years. Every format number lives in [`config/sections.js`](config/sections.js) and [`config/presets.js`](config/presets.js) with its source. If your online assessment looked different, [open a format report](https://github.com/Coflazo/optprep/issues/new?template=format-report.yml) and the next release will match it.
-- Zap-Q, the personality questionnaire, has no right answers to practise. Answer it honestly and consistently.
-- Software engineering, quant developer and quant researcher assessments are different tests. OptPrep does not cover them.
-
-## How the questions are checked
-
-Generators have JavaScript checks. The optional Python/C++ pipeline checks the structure of every exported item and independently recalculates only the supported numerical scenarios. Its report distinguishes numerical coverage, skipped checks, warnings and failures:
-
-- Orderbooks: every board re-solved by a C++ branch and bound.
-- NumberLogic: supported next-term items are searched by a C++ rule library; missing-term skips and unsupported-rule warnings are reported separately.
-- Beat the Odds: supported model variants are re-simulated by C++ Monte Carlo.
-- Likelihood List: supported fund-return, score-table, conjunction and Markov scenarios are independently recomputed in Python; other scenarios remain explicitly unchecked by this second-language pipeline.
-- Intervals: supported scenarios are recomputed from their data in Python or simulated in C++; numerical coverage is reported.
-- Zap-N: optimal play re-solved in C++ for Skyscraper, NumberBox, Figure It Out and Balloon.
